{:edb/db-before-t 1686 :edb/db-after-t 1687 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534999 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:50:41.892Z" 13194139534999 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325717 13194139534999 true] [17592186198200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325724 13194139534999 true] [17592186198201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325710 13194139534999 true] [17592186198202 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325703 13194139534999 true] [17592186198733 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534999 true] [17592186198733 1100 "Which of the following represents the set $S = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534999 false] [17592186198733 1100 "Which of the following represents the set $S=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534999 true] [17592186198733 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325704 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1089 "q-177649" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1100 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1113 #uuid "baf40def-809e-5a8c-bd6e-f8be8448a5df" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325703 1145 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325705 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325706 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325707 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325708 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325709 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1041 "selection" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325709 13194139534999 true] [17592186325704 1110 #uuid "f1ff8b86-ea5e-533e-9e3a-2e68d8c8e7a4" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325705 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/ebd57b72a8e825778bce33f84bcbec2a0bd8d852a5b2ce25a707224b39d07f99.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325705 1111 #uuid "e07bdf4f-6187-5516-b017-6bb33da91de1" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325706 1034 "$∅,$ the empty set" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534999 true] [17592186325706 1111 #uuid "b35691b3-c33a-57a7-9c1f-b43026489905" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325707 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/0d4597773001223e64f19e1db23c441cc7d4ffb1481c564b63f456fa05e4a0ed.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325707 1111 #uuid "1e8b8a81-5f08-5d6b-8679-b5c9663abb68" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325708 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/33c6eba539c7bc77e4c349c444da281fb3945aeb9f48018986e8c6373bc0e3b5.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325708 1111 #uuid "d76c1d48-acd7-5ac5-9e3b-8f58292addf5" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325709 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325709 1111 #uuid "8059b462-6b05-5b2e-b2c5-aef0c7b76978" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325711 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1089 "q-177650" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1100 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {i}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1113 #uuid "62556693-fd48-5895-851c-d4aab5200ee0" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325710 1145 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(0,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/7587daeaea45c5ef3449ecb676133c60bbedb7819308783bac835f3ae9c6883c.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times {i}={(k,i)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(0,i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/b926204a3d58b25889f1c79dd0f65c1ea990c67bc88e869004396a77970e8dd4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png)" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325712 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325713 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325714 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325715 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325716 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1041 "selection" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325713 13194139534999 true] [17592186325711 1110 #uuid "6eab96d1-a181-526e-a01d-0369f3b13504" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325712 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/98077ef737a2487f9e480b7ffd42376785c5681e2651b98e7b71abb4e645d876.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325712 1111 #uuid "ab251298-825c-5651-9c5b-498e30a84797" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325713 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/4a81de1a698eedf6a636d66b68a7c62efa69d1d026a961ae97ab5ecc2ef9828d.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325713 1111 #uuid "c9b9de57-7448-5069-bc6d-824054bab476" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325714 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/749f4fb3a6416147cdd69c08bf806c28e2112929453bb8ae405c48452d82126b.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325714 1111 #uuid "7ee24989-0864-55f6-a02d-4890b410f519" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325715 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/aaa04c2578557d03dd144c27e6a5c223126830ba2a3f8288adc9239daf604e55.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325715 1111 #uuid "7cd0815e-1533-5591-8a51-8db7447b6810" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325716 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/36b36ac05cb1054e198f2e40a2d0ffdd40e952abaa8ddf5c946a38fde946adc8.png" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534999 true] [17592186325716 1111 #uuid "bf04a8dc-3ccb-56f6-881e-bda5ca60a731" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325718 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1089 "q-181779" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1100 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=N\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1113 #uuid "4bfdf4e4-11aa-513b-b13d-ea378cad8242" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325717 1145 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=N\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in N,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/067b3b386273b09d5614f934f6525bd3e43ff46f420eb8aa7e744f635ff6d101.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/a870f65350ad00d258471079a34bec49a47d331c176dc7a91b3c7aae5ae968dd.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044579/assets/c0df23ec47d729f597c02acff1530dd646a6f6c506a6497a8cb4d75b7fce5e74.png)" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325719 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325720 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325721 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325722 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325723 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1041 "selection" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325723 13194139534999 true] [17592186325718 1110 #uuid "dd6d0ccc-c30d-50da-8f69-63f8dff8461c" 13194139534999 true] [17592186325719 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044